window.docContents[184].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Equation-Specific-Branches":{"contents":"The nonlinear heat branch begins on a bounded domain, where a first\nDirichlet eigenfunction moment excludes nonnegative weak solutions with large\ninitial data on a prescribed interval. On the whole space it contains a\ncomplete example: a nonnegative spatially constant classical solution is\nverified directly and its spatial supremum norm is proved to blow up at finite\ntime. The same chapter derives Type I and scaling-invariant profile equations.\nThese profile theorems say when an ansatz solves the PDE and when its scale\nforces concentration; the profile itself is a hypothesis.\n\nThe parabolic Keller–Segel branch derives the density and potential profile\nequations under the standard similarity scaling. A pair of  profiles\nsatisfying both equations, with nonnegative density profile, generates a\nclassical pre-singular solution. A bounded density profile with one nonzero\nvalue makes the density ansatz blow up in the spatial supremum norm.\n\nThe virial branch follows a different route. An affine second-moment identity\nand nonnegativity constrain the right endpoint of a solution interval by an\nexplicit horizon. This is a lifespan theorem: it rules out continuation of a\nsolution retaining the stated moment properties beyond that time.\n\nThe abnormal three-dimensional scaling is analyzed as an obstruction\ncalculation. Its amplitude diverges and its diffusion coefficient vanishes\nin rescaled variables, but the physical residual can diverge. Under the\naggregation profile equation, the exact criterion says that the density\nresidual vanishes everywhere precisely when the density profile is harmonic.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.3. Equation-Specific Branches","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Equation-Specific-Branches"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-PDE-Theorem-API":{"contents":"\n\nTo check a proposed heat profile, we hold space fixed while differentiating\nin time, then hold time fixed while taking the spatial Laplacian. In the\nlibrary's spacetime coordinates , time is coordinate zero, so the\ncalculation is\n\n\n\nFor the Gaussian the domain matters, since its formula contains ; the\npolynomial  has no such restriction. In both cases cas\ncomputes the coordinate residual, and the slice-Laplacian theorem identifies\nthe spatial sum with the Laplacian of . This is how\nthe calculation enters the ordinary heat-equation predicate.\n\nThe remaining examples ask different questions of the same calculus.\nHarmonic kernels require a punctured domain and a normalization; transport\nadds prescribed values at time zero; rigid rotation tests a symmetric\ngradient rather than a scalar Laplacian. Each theorem states which of these\nproperties has been proved.\n\nThe field itself can remain abstract. In\nPDE/Examples/Symbolic/CAS/FluxDerivatives.lean, if  is differentiable at\n, , and  is a fixed vector, the reciprocal-flux example\nproves .\nHere cas differentiates the scalar reciprocal at , and the general\ndivergence chain rule supplies the dependence on . Only the stated\npointwise hypotheses are needed.\n\nThe symbolically represented planar Gaussian ansatz, interpreted as an ordinary\nspacetime function, has zero heat residual in spacetime coordinates at positive\ntime, and the CAS proves it. Withthe linked Lean theorem statesat . The slice-Laplacian identity turns this into the residual\npredicate\n.\nFor the analytic kernel that predicate is the\nGaussian heat theorem; for the\nsymbolic expression the Lean module derives it in an example.\n\nWrite  and . Differentiating the prefactor\n and the exponential givesAdding the two spatial derivatives recovers the time derivative. The CAS\nchecks this cancellation. The assumption  discharges its time\ndenominators and the validity conditions needed to interpret both rounds of\ndifferentiation;  handles the constant denominator. The residual\nbridge then gives the displayed equation for the coordinate partial\nderivatives.Positive time also selects the branch on which this formula agrees with\nthe analytic heat kernel, whose definition is zero for . At the\nspatial origin the positive-time value is , which grows without\nbound as . Thus substituting the definition's value at time\nzero cannot establish an initial-trace theorem.\n\nThe two-dimensional Gaussian heat kernel satisfies the analytic heat equation\non a domain where time is positive. The theorem statement uses the ordinary\nheat-equation predicate defined in the parabolic API. Its proof comes from\nthe analytic heat-kernel theorem; the CAS computation of the same identity in\nspacetime coordinates is the\nsymbolic Gaussian bridge.For every ,\n\nThe positive-time assumption supplies the nonzero and positivity conditions\nneeded when differentiating the Gaussian factor. The heat-kernel calculation\nthen identifies its time derivative with its spatial Laplacian. Rewriting the\nheat residual by that identity gives zero at every point of the stated domain,\nwhich is the SatisfiesPDEProblem conclusion.\n\nThe polynomial profilehas zero residual for the ordinary two-dimensional heat-equation problem on\nall spacetime; the linked SatisfiesPDEProblem theorem is exactly that\nresidual property.\n\nHere , , and , so the checked residual is\n. Polynomial evaluation and its derivatives are valid everywhere;\nthe coordinate and fixed-time slice bridges therefore apply on all\nspacetime.\n\nThe linked value bridges identify the logarithmic planar expression with the\nnormalized two-dimensional fundamental solution and the three-dimensional\nfundamental solution with a constant multiple of the Newtonian core. The\nsymbolic profiles are checked to be harmonic away from the pole, with domain\nhypotheses kept explicit.For the Poisson-normalized kernelsthe linked theorems prove\n\nFor the planar logarithmic expression, \ntransports the checked CAS residual to the analytic Laplacian. The Newtonian\ncore instead reuses the general radial-Laplacian identity: the coefficient\n vanishes at , . Its value bridge identifies the\nreflected expression with the inverse norm; constant scaling gives the\nnormalized fundamental solution.\n\nHarmonicity away from the pole cannot determine the normalization: every\nconstant multiple of a harmonic profile is still harmonic there. In three\ndimensions the unit-source normalization is visible in the flux through a\nsphere of radius :\n\n\n\nThis calculation explains why the coefficient  matters. A\ndistributional identity  must account for that\nflux at the excluded pole, which the pointwise zero-residual theorem does not\nsee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.4. Symbolic PDE Theorem API","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-PDE-Theorem-API"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Differentiation___-Gradients___-and-Laplacians":{"contents":"Consider the scalar function . To differentiate only in\nthe first coordinate, write:\n\n-- Differentiate in x; y remains a parameter.\n#cas [derive x] (sin (x * y))\n\n\nThe compact report says:\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin (x * y))\nOutput (scalar ℝ): cos(x*y)*y\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe domain line identifies the ordered real coordinate space inferred from\nthe free variables. The input and output lines identify scalar-valued\nexpressions; the output is the partial derivative , not the\nfull derivative of a one-variable function. With set_option cas.verbose true,\nMore details also shows the sine chain rule, the product rule for , the raw expression\n, and the checked normalization to\n.\n\nFor the full gradient, name its coordinates and their order:\n\n-- The written variable order fixes the\n-- output coordinate order.\n#cas [grad x, y] (sin (x * y))\n\n\nThe result is\n\n#cas [grad x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin (x * y))\nOutput (vector ℝ^2; order x, y): (cos(x*y)*y, cos(x*y)*x)\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThus grad x, y means\n. Writing grad y, x requests the same two\npartials in the opposite output order. This explicit convention avoids\nguessing whether a multivariable derive request meant one coordinate or an\nentire gradient.\n\nThe Laplacian computes each diagonal second partial and sums the results:\n\n-- Sum the diagonal second derivatives in x and y.\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2)\n\n\n#cas [laplacian x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 4\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe final bridge theorem identifies the assembled reflected expression with\nMathlib's analytic gradient or the analytic Laplacian defined in the\nfoundations chapter.\n\nA real power is differentiated by the rule behind Mathlib's\nHasDerivAt.rpow, and the report records the positivity of the base:\n\n-- A real exponent; the base must be positive.\n#cas [derive x] (x ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ))\n\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ))\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 3/2*sqrt(x)\nConditions: x > 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe raw derivative is ; the normal form reads the\nliteral exponent  back as a square root. A symbolic exponent stays a\nreal power, so #cas [derive x] (x ^ y) reports  under the\nsame condition.\n\nThe theorem tactic can also differentiate functions outside the calculator's real-expression\ngrammar. Mathlib supplies the arithmetic and chain rules; derivative families in the local\ncontext supply the function-specific step:\n\nexample (f f' : ℝ → ℝ) (hf : ∀ x, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) (x : ℝ) :\n    deriv (fun t ↦ f (t ^ 2) + f t) x = f' (x ^ 2) * (2 * x) + f' x := by\n  cas\n\n\nA theorem tagged @[cas_deriv] makes its derivative rule available to subsequent calls.\nThe rule's premises still have to be proved. This route also handles complex scalar calculus:\n\nexample (z : ℂ) :\n    HasDerivAt (fun w : ℂ ↦ Complex.exp (w ^ 2)) (2 * z * Complex.exp (z ^ 2)) z := by\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.3. Differentiation, Gradients, and Laplacians","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Differentiation___-Gradients___-and-Laplacians"}});